# Episode deliverables

## Purpose

Deliver each webtoon episode in the form platforms and phones need: segments
at the right width, slices that upload cleanly, lettering that reads on a
small screen, and the thumbnail and cover that sell the episode.

## Scope

Applies only when `comic_format` in `production/project.yaml` is `webtoon`.
Governs the `page` and `lettering` blocks and the `deliverables` of
`project.yaml`, the editions built for each episode, and the files prepared
from them for upload. Sits on top of `rules/common/` and the domain layers,
and narrows `publishing/export-formats.md` for vertical-scroll delivery
without contradicting it.

## Core Principles

- An episode is an issue. It has an `ISS_###` ID, its own pages, its own
  continuity state, and its own edition.
- The phone decides. Every choice about size is tested at the width of a
  phone held in one hand.
- Platform limits are facts to look up. The numbers here are defaults, and
  the platform's current specification wins.
- Thumbnails and covers are deliverables with the same discipline as pages.

## Episode as issue

- Each episode is one entry in `story/series-plan.yaml` and one
  `issues/<ISS_ID>/` directory. A project of this kind sets
  `publication: webtoon-series`.
- `page_budget` counts scroll segments. Default episode length: 10 to 15
  segments, or 40 to 60 panels. Short episodes on a steady schedule serve
  readers better than long ones that slip.
- Each episode ends where the reader will want the next one (see
  `scroll-grammar.md`), and opens with enough orientation for someone who
  read the previous episode a week ago: a place, a face, the open question.
- `issue.opens_from` names the continuity state of the previous episode.
  State carries across episodes exactly as it does across issues.
- An episode is published from an edition (`ED_<issue>_##`), under the rules
  of `publishing/edition-assembly.md`. A corrected episode is a new edition.

## Segment size and slicing

- Segments are generated at the project's `page.width_px` and
  `page.height_px`, in a tall ratio the provider supports (`9:16` by
  default). Work at twice the delivery width: 1600 px wide for an 800 px
  delivery.
- Default delivery: slices 800 px wide and at most 1280 px tall, as JPEG or
  PNG, each under 2 MB. Many platforms use these or similar limits. Record
  the target platform's real numbers in the `notes` of the project's
  deliverable.
- Slice boundaries fall in gutters. A slice never cuts through a face, a
  balloon, or a line of lettering, because platforms load slices one by one
  and a slow connection shows the cut.
- Slices are named in reading order with zero-padded numbers, and carry the
  edition ID.
- The scaffold does not slice or downscale. `scripts/build-edition.js`
  builds the `web`, `cbz`, and `pdf` exports from the full segments, and
  slicing to platform size is done in an image tool from the renders of one
  named edition (`publishing/export-formats.md`).
- Downscale once, from the full-size segment to the delivery width. Do not
  upscale a segment generated too small.

## Mobile-first legibility

- Read every segment at phone width before it is reviewed: the image scaled
  to about 400 px wide. Lettering that needs zooming fails.
- Default minimum size for dialogue: 3% of the column width as font size
  (24 px at 800 px, 48 px at 1600 px). Captions and whispers may go slightly
  smaller, and nothing goes below 2.5%.
- Tighten the word budgets for the format. Suggested values in
  `project.yaml`: `lettering.max_words_per_balloon: 20` and
  `lettering.max_words_per_panel: 40`. Lines break often, at three to five
  words.
- Keep lettering clear of the outer 5% of the column on each side. Phones
  have rounded corners and cases, and platforms overlay controls.
- Contrast carries on a small, bright screen in daylight. Balloons have a
  solid fill, and text placed directly on art gets an outline or a patch.
- When the provider's text rendering is unreliable at this density, use
  `overlay` lettering (`image/image-generation.md`).

## Thumbnail and episode cover

- Each episode ships with a thumbnail, and each series with a cover. Their
  sizes and ratios come from the platform. Record them in the `notes` of
  the project's deliverable.
- A thumbnail is seen at about the size of a postage stamp. One face or one
  object, strong contrast, no small text, and no spoiler for the episode's
  ending.
- A thumbnail cropped from a reviewed render is derived work, done in an
  image tool. The edition's `notes` name the source render and the crop.
- A cover or title card drawn for the episode is planned as a page of the
  issue, usually its first segment with a single `splash` panel. It then
  gets a page file, a prompt package with the look master attached, a
  versioned render, and a review like any other page.
- A series cover is planned the same way, as a page of the episode it
  launches with. The data model has no separate slot for cover art, and a
  cover made outside the pipeline would have no prompt package, no record,
  and no review.
- The identity checks apply. A cover that shows a character off-model
  misleads every reader who clicks on it.

## Validation

- `schemas/project.schema.json` restricts `comic_format`, `publication`, and
  `deliverables[].target` to their allowed values and checks the `page` and
  `lettering` blocks.
- `scripts/build-edition.js` and `scripts/validate-workspace.js` apply the
  same review and edition gates to an episode as to any issue: listed
  renders need a `pass` or `fixed` review outside a draft, and exports are
  not overwritten.
- Slice dimensions, file sizes, and file names are produced outside the
  scaffold. The prepress-specialist checks them against the platform's
  specification before upload and records the result in the edition's
  `notes`.
- Legibility at phone width cannot be read from a file. It is part of the
  `lettering` check of the visual review, performed on the segment scaled
  to phone width.
- Thumbnails and covers are judged by the art-director against the character
  sheets and the look master. A cover planned as a page is covered by that
  page's review.
